\subsection{Episode-Level Training: Seed Variability}
\label{sec:seed_variability}

Episode-level GRPO resolves the dual-mechanism failure, but training outcomes remain seed-dependent. We systematically evaluate $N{=}11$ random seeds under identical hyperparameters, revealing a $2{\times}2$ structure: \textit{training stability} (collapsed vs.\ non-collapsed) $\times$ \textit{OOD generalization} (stagnant vs.\ generalized).

We define \textbf{collapsed} seeds as those where in-distribution success declines $>$50\% from peak and remains below baseline for $\geq$3 consecutive steps. \textbf{Non-collapsed} seeds are the complement. Among non-collapsed seeds, we further distinguish \textbf{generalized} (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT on the 134-task OOD set) from \textbf{stagnant} (not significant vs.\ SFT). Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} reports results for each seed at its best-performing checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Episode-level GRPO across 11 seeds (134 OOD tasks, $\tau{=}0.4$). Category: C=Collapsed, S=Non-collapsed (stagnant), G=Generalized. Wilson 95\% CI. SFT baseline: 6.72\% (9/134).}
\label{tab:seed_variability}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Step} & \textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{vs SFT} \\
\midrule
seed2048 & 5 & C & 8.21 & [4.6, 14.2] & NS \\
seed42 & 5 & C & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed8 & 5 & C & 13.43 & [8.6, 20.3] & NS \\
\midrule
seed200 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed300 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed100 & 5 & S & 11.19 & [6.9, 17.6] & NS \\
seed100 & 10 & S & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed2 & 15 & S & 15.67 & [10.5, 22.8] & $p{=}0.02$ \\
\midrule
seed1 & 5 & G$^\dagger$ & 23.88 & [17.4, 31.8] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed5 & 10 & G & 25.37 & [18.8, 33.3] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed6 & 15 & G & 29.10 & [22.2, 37.1] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed4 & 15 & G & 30.60 & [23.5, 38.7] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\begin{flushleft}
\small $^\dagger$ RAGEN built-in eval; checkpoint unavailable for re-evaluation.
\end{flushleft}
\end{table}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Training dynamics of episode-level GRPO across 9 seeds. (a)~In-distribution success rate. (b)~Response length. Red: collapsed seeds (2048, 8, 42); orange: non-collapsed but stagnant (100, 200, 300); green: generalized (4, 5, 6); blue dashed: delayed learner (seed~2). Gray dashed line: SFT baseline (14\%).}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure*}

Three outcomes emerge (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}): 3/11 seeds collapse (27\%), with in-distribution success dropping below baseline and response length inflating to 14{,}000--16{,}000 tokens. 4/11 seeds are non-collapsed but stagnant (36\%): training succeeds (in-distribution improvement) but OOD performance remains statistically indistinguishable from SFT (10.45--15.67\%, all NS at $\alpha{=}0.01$). 3/11 seeds generalize (27\%), achieving 25.37--30.60\% OOD with Fisher exact $p{<}0.001$. One seed (seed2) exhibits delayed learning, reaching 15.67\% OOD only after 15 steps ($p{=}0.02$, borderline).% exp_id: exp029 multi-seed series

The stagnant--generalized gap is not explained by insufficient training: seed100 improves only 1.50pp from step~5 to step~10 (11.19\% $\to$ 12.69\%, both NS), while seed5 reaches 25.37\% at step~10. Stable training is necessary but not sufficient for OOD generalization---the mechanism driving seed-dependent transfer remains an open question (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).

